NNAAMMEE
     eexxppaanndd, uunneexxppaanndd — expand tabs to spaces, and vice versa

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     eexxppaanndd [--tt _t_a_b_l_i_s_t] [_f_i_l_e _._._.]
     uunneexxppaanndd [--aa] [_f_i_l_e _._._.]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     eexxppaanndd processes the named files or the standard input, writing the stan‐
     dard output with tabs changed into blanks.  Backspace characters are pre‐
     served into the output and decrement the column count for tab calcula‐
     tions.  eexxppaanndd is useful for pre-processing character files (before sort‐
     ing, looking at specific columns, etc.) that contain tabs.

     uunneexxppaanndd puts tabs back into the data from the standard input or the
     named files and writes the result on the standard output.

     The options are as follows:

     --aa    By default, only leading blanks and tabs are reconverted to maximal
           strings of tabs.  If the --aa option is given, tabs are inserted
           whenever they would compress the resultant file by replacing two or
           more characters.

     --tt _t_a_b_l_i_s_t
           If the argument to --tt, _t_a_b_l_i_s_t, consists of a single numerical ar‐
           gument, tabs are set _t_a_b_l_i_s_t spaces apart instead of the default 8.
           If multiple --tt options are given, then the tabs are set at those
           specific columns.

           Otherwise the argument to --tt may consist of a list of two or more
           positive decimal integers, separated by commas or single blank
           characters, in ascending order: in the event of having to process a
           ⟨tab⟩ at a position beyond the last of those specified in a multi‐
           ple --tt list, the ⟨tab⟩ is replaced by a single ⟨space⟩ character.

           This implementation of eexxppaanndd additionally allows _t_a_b_l_i_s_t to be
           specified without using the --tt flag, by prefixing _t_a_b_l_i_s_t with a
           dash (-).

EEXXIITT SSTTAATTUUSS
     The eexxppaanndd and uunneexxppaanndd utilities exit 0 on success, and >0 if an error
     occurs.

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     fold(1)

SSTTAANNDDAARRDDSS
     The eexxppaanndd and uunneexxppaanndd utilities are compliant with the IEEE Std
     1003.1-2008 (“POSIX.1”) specification, except that this implementation
     does not support a --tt flag for uunneexxppaanndd.

     The ability to specify tabstops without the --tt flag is not specified by
     IEEE Std 1003.1-2008 (“POSIX.1”), though it states it “may be present in
     some implementations”.

HHIISSTTOORRYY
     The eexxppaanndd utility first appeared in 1BSD.

